 /*
- * METROID / Nothing Phone (3) internal UFS root device.
- *
- * Experimental bare-metal handoff driver. UFSHCI version and MCQ/legacy
- * availability must be checked at runtime. No controller reset/PHY re-init
- * is performed. Only use with a validated DMA identity mapping; a boot argument
- * is required to opt into physical-address DMA until an SMMU DMA mapper exists.
- * A valid UTRL base register readback is NOT evidence of a working DMA mapping.
- * Commands use polled legacy single-doorbell mode only,
- * one at a time, to LUN 0. It finds one GPT partition by name (boot-arg
- * ufsroot=, default "userdata") and exposes it as the BSD disk ufsroot0
- * (rufsroot0), with 512-byte logical sectors over the device's 4 KiB blocks.
- * Root on it with rd=ufsroot0.
- *
- * DMA uses one physically contiguous allocation and a 1 MiB bounce buffer.
- * Device-tree marks the UFS endpoint dma-coherent, but firmware SMMU mappings
- * remain unknown. Cache maintenance is inherited from the experimental code
- * and needs target-XNU validation. Writes are disabled by default.
- * Experimental writes additionally require ufsrw=1 and an exact
- * ufsrw_partition= name for a dedicated metroid_/xnu_ GPT partition.
- * Never enable writes on an Android-owned partition or without a verified
- * SMMU DMA mapping; these flags do not make an unsafe controller safe.
+ * Nothing Phone (3) UFS root adapter, backed by an SMMUv2 DMA domain.
+ * Transport sequencing follows Nothing's SM8735 Linux driver; XNU retains
+ * ownership of allocation, serialization and BSD device publication.
+ * Firmware must leave PHY/clocks and a legacy-capable host powered.
  */
